                <pb facs="0183" n="1017" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1017"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/7018</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sharp</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-02-19T11:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 19, 1918, 11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4.32 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3214. Note received from Foreign Office stating that it attaches great importance
                    to closer cooperation among principal Allies in preparing, establishing, and
                    applying black list and in order to avoid delays in exchange of communications
                    between these Governments it desires that an American, French, and English
                    representative be designated in each capital to discuss with competent services
                    all questions relating to the black list. The French Government requests, in
                    case proposition is adopted, that a member of Embassy be designated to enter
                    into direct relations with service of black lists of Ministry of Blockade. I
                    would appreciate being informed of reply which I may make. Foreign Office note
                    follows by next pouch.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
